## 7. Comparative Analysis & Engineering Trade-Offs

### Inheritance-based Spot vs Strategy-based Spot Fit

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Inheritance (`CompactSpot`, `LargeSpot`)** | Strong type safety | Hard to dynamically reconfigure spot sizes |
| **Enum Attribute (`spotType`)** | Flexible, spots can be re-dimensioned | Requires switch/if evaluation |

## 8. Common Pitfalls & Antipatterns

### Pitfall 1: Putting all parking and payment logic in one giant ParkingLot God class
- **Why developers fail here:** Violates SRP and OCP. Adding a new payment processor or parking spot type breaks the entire class.
- **Concrete Counterexample / Symptom:** A 2,000 line ParkingLot.cpp file with dozens of nested if-else statements.
- **How to prevent / correct:** Decompose into Floor, Spot, Ticket, Gate, and IPricingStrategy components.

## 9. Interview Questions & Comprehensive Answers

### Q1: How do you handle thread-safety when two cars enter two gates simultaneously looking for spots? (Concurrency in LLD)

**Model Answer:**
Use mutex synchronization around the spot allocation manager. Each floor or section can have its own fine-grained mutex (e.g., std::mutex floorMutex) so cars entering Floor 1 do not block cars parking on Floor 2, avoiding global contention.

*Interviewer Follow-up:* "How would you implement dynamic pricing?"

*Follow-up Response:* Inject an IPricingStrategy interface into the payment calculator. A PeakHoursPricing strategy can scale rates based on current parking occupancy percentage.

## 10. Rapid Revision Cheat Sheet

- **LLD Approach:** 1. Clarify Requirements -> 2. Define Entities -> 3. Class Diagram -> 4. Implement contracts
- **Parking Lot:** Vehicle, Spot, Ticket, Gate, Floor, PricingStrategy
- **Thread Safety:** Protect spot allocation with fine-grained floor mutexes

## 11. Self-Assessment Mastery Checklist

- [ ] Can structure and write a complete Low-Level Design in C++
- [ ] Can apply Strategy and Factory patterns to real-world domain problems
- [ ] Addresses concurrency and edge cases in design interviews
